An element's disabled state and popup for assistive technology (GPUI patch for
docs/plans/20261031-e2e-ui-and-coverage.md Task 6): `StatefulInteractiveElement::aria_disabled` sets AccessKit's
`disabled` flag on the element's node and `aria_has_popup` its `has_popup`, neither of which GPUI offered a way to set,
so a dimmed and inert control reads as disabled (AT-SPI without ENABLED, UIA `IsEnabled` false, macOS `AXEnabled`
false) and a button that opens a menu reads as a menu button. An upstream candidate (Zed): new builders, no behavior
change for elements that do not call them.
scripts/vendor-gpui.sh applies this after 0004.

diff -ruN a/src/elements/div.rs b/src/elements/div.rs
--- a/src/elements/div.rs
+++ b/src/elements/div.rs
@@ -1408,6 +1408,20 @@
         self
     }
 
+    /// Report this element as disabled: shown but not operable (AT-SPI clears its
+    /// ENABLED and SENSITIVE states, UIA reports `IsEnabled` false, macOS
+    /// `AXEnabled` false).
+    fn aria_disabled(mut self, disabled: bool) -> Self {
+        self.interactivity().aria.disabled = disabled;
+        self
+    }
+
+    /// Report the kind of popup this element opens (a menu button's menu).
+    fn aria_has_popup(mut self, popup: accesskit::HasPopup) -> Self {
+        self.interactivity().aria.has_popup = Some(popup);
+        self
+    }
+
     /// Set the numeric value for this element.
     fn aria_numeric_value(mut self, value: f64) -> Self {
         self.interactivity().aria.numeric_value = Some(value);
